From: fexdroid
Subject: [PATCH] tu/kgsl: answer zero-timeout timestamp waits as polls

KGSL treats a timeout of 0 in IOCTL_KGSL_DEVICE_WAITTIMESTAMP_CTXTID as "wait
forever". get_relative_ms() returns 0 once the deadline has passed (and for
callers that only poll), so every poll blocked until the GPU retired the
timestamp, keeping CPU and GPU in lock-step. Read the retired timestamp
instead and compare it with wrap-around.

Idea credit: Bannerlator / DroidDeck (kgslpoll). Written independently for
fexdroid; no code copied. License: MIT (Mesa).
---
diff -ruN a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_knl_kgsl.cc b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_knl_kgsl.cc
--- a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_knl_kgsl.cc	2026-10-11 12:20:26.626556865 +0300
+++ b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_knl_kgsl.cc	2026-10-11 12:20:26.640556956 +0300
@@ -840,6 +840,23 @@
       .timeout = get_relative_ms(abs_timeout_ns),
    };
 
+   /* KGSL reads a timeout of 0 as "no timeout", so a zero-timeout wait (a
+    * poll: "has this retired yet?") would block until the GPU gets there and
+    * keep the CPU in lock-step with the GPU. Answer polls from the retired
+    * timestamp instead.
+    */
+   if (wait.timeout == 0) {
+      struct kgsl_cmdstream_readtimestamp_ctxtid rd = {
+         .context_id = context_id,
+         .type = KGSL_TIMESTAMP_RETIRED,
+      };
+      if (ioctl(fd, IOCTL_KGSL_CMDSTREAM_READTIMESTAMP_CTXTID, &rd) == 0)
+         return timestamp_cmp(rd.timestamp, timestamp) ? VK_SUCCESS
+                                                         : VK_TIMEOUT;
+      /* Unknown to the read: fall back to a 1 ms wait, never an endless one. */
+      wait.timeout = 1;
+   }
+
    while (true) {
       int ret = ioctl(fd, IOCTL_KGSL_DEVICE_WAITTIMESTAMP_CTXTID, &wait);
 
